  "body": "Conjecture [aa0fe674450fa087] misreads the rule; the code does not part company with the rule as written. The rule says (source [3145b9e536590f37]) \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\" and \"A candidate that parses as strict JSON, or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable; one that does not (a float, for instance) is passed over\". The fenced span in each reply is the single candidate '{\"a\":1}\\n3.14' (or with a=2). That candidate does not parse as strict JSON or last-wins JSON, so it is passed over. The rule then falls to the else branch: the last top-level object in the text is {\"c\":3}. The code returns {'c':3} for both replies, exactly as the rule's else branch requires. The conjecture instead treats the fenced span as a container of multiple candidates and selects the last scoreable object inside it, {\"a\":1}/{\"a\":2}; that is not what the rule says, because the rule's fenced candidate is the whole fenced span, not each top-level object within it. The observation the conjecture names—same answer for both replies—is what the code returns, but it does not show the code violates the rule; it shows the conjecture misread the rule.\n\nReading [6334f45feca202d1] is defective, not the conjecture. It says \"No uncertainty; the conjecture is clear\" and commits only \"expect\": \"moves\". But the conjecture explicitly states \"The rule therefore requires the check to return {\"a\":1} for reply one and {\"a\":2} for reply two.\" The reading's \"moves\" is a weaker property that would be satisfied by any differing pair, so it fails to render the specific expected values. The prose names exact outputs; the reading abstracts them away. The reading names the correct kernel and the rewrite is faithful, but the expectation is under-specified.",
